From c6066348b8f8c32c2f650f386595386724db344c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 21:40:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gallium/pvr: multiple render targets - one PBE emit per
 colour attachment

ES2 + GL_EXT_draw_buffers needs several colour outputs, and the open driver had only ever written
one. The PBE writes a colour target at end of tile through an EMITPIX in the end-of-tile (EOT)
program, so MRT means one emit per attachment, each with its own PBE state words - exactly what the
Vulkan driver's pvr_usc_eot() generates. Build the EOT program from NIR with one EMITPIX per
attachment (a WOP between them so the PBE processes them in order, freep on the last one, which owns
the pixel), and give emit i the state words of attachment i: its address, format, mrt_index (the
index into the job's PBE register-word table) and source_pos (the pixel output dword it reads:
START_BIT0/32/64/96). The fragment variant maps FRAG_RESULT_DATA0..3 onto output dwords 0..3 and the
per-job PBE register-word table describes each attachment's surface; the background clear program
stores one value per output so a clear covers every attachment. A render only becomes an MRT render
when it really is the framebuffer's (materialize/blit renders open their own target).

The one non-obvious failure this hit: nir_lower_blend deletes a colour store whose colormask is zero,
and Mesa's state tracker zero-fills pipe_blend_state::rt[] past the entries the API configured, so
attachment 1's store was deleted before pco ever saw it. ES2 has a single blend state and a single
glColorMask, applied to every draw buffer, so every attachment now uses render target 0's state.

Verified with zink as the reference (same EGL surfaceless harness, open-perf/mrt_test.c:
2x RGBA8, gl_FragData[0]=red gl_FragData[1]=blue, then samples each attachment and reads the pixel):
- attached draw: attachment0 255 0 0 255, attachment1 0 0 255 255 - zink gives the same two pixels;
- glClear covers both attachments (64 128 191 255 in both);
- a second MRT draw overwrites both (attachment0 green, attachment1 yellow);
- no-clear draw writes both attachments; the old failure (attachment1 stale) is gone;
- full gltri gate: 49 modes, every native mode glGetError 0x0; rttest PASS (bad=0/262144);
  rt8test unchanged (255 0 / 255 128); 0 MMU faults.
Known limitations: v1 requires every attachment to share attachment 0's format (mixed sets fall back
to attachment 0 with a warning); per-render-target blend state does not exist in ES2 and is not
tracked, and tile buffers are not used.
